cies have had a devastating  impact on his ability to govern and  manage his caucus he fears that those  conspiracy theories are now feeding a  deep alienation cutting some albertans  off from appeals based in fact or Reason  and he's not sure how to come back from  that I found through covet an inability  to communicate  basic facts to a certain element of the  population and I don't know how to  remedy that well Danielle Smith appealed  to the more radical elements of what  Kenny called Q anon-like conspiracy  theories in her campaign the question is  if she will now moderate to appeal to a  broader cross-sectional of albertans  whatever is next for Premier design Nick  Danielle Smith it is clear that her main  target will be Ottawa and prime minister  Justin Trudeau the provinces are now  beginning to realize that we are not a  subordinate level of government to  Ottawa and Ottawa should be treating us  with the same respect that they treat  Quebec that's all we're asking for  there's no question that there is anger  in Alberta for Smith to tap into in a  province where many have felt ignored  and disrespected but several of Smith's  promises exceed the power of a premier  their Federal territory the question now  is whether the appeal of a champion  against Ottawa will be enough to see the  UCP re-elected in May or does Smith run  the risk of using the same rhetorical  Gambit that got Jason Kenney in trouble  making promises without deliverables and  facing an outraged electorate pharah  Mercedes Stevenson in Calgary thank you  Mercedes and you can watch the whole  interview with Jason Kenney on the west  block tomorrow morning right here on  global  I had breaking barriers meet the  Canadian medical association's first  indigenous president his plans to change  this country's unequal Health Care  system  [Music]  the first indigenous leader of the  Canadian Medical Association is new in  the job but as the president of the  largest group representing doctors in  this country Dr Alika LaFontaine has his  work cut out for him the  anesthesiologist from Grand Prairie  Alberta has spent many years of his  20-year career helping marginalized  Canadians navigate the Health Care  system and advocating for their care  tonight on the new reality Anita Garcia  speaks with Dr LaFontaine and she joins  us now with more nitu when Dr Alika  LaFontaine first stepped into this new  role he wanted to ensure that people in  leadership positions could see the  barriers to health care that so many  Canadians face and because he's dealt  with it firsthand too he knows it's a  deeply ingrained issue in Canadian  medicine the experience of racism Israel  I've experienced it myself I have family  members who've experienced it and once  we can acknowledge that things are real  we can then work towards those solutions  that I think are in the hands of the  people that experience them  adequate Access to Health Care is  something many indigenous peoples face  across the country some even fear seeing  a doctor we have this history in Canada  where we've been taught to look at  indigenous people certain ways and we've  internalized that within the Healthcare  System organizations like the Canadian  Medical Association that have been  around for 155 years you know they're on  their own path towards reconciliation  you know we've never had more indigenous  physicians in this country than we've  had now and we have a lot of people who  you know are acting as allies trying to  create spaces for these conversations 10  tens of thousands of doctors are members  of the Canadian Medical Association the  largest advocacy group in Canada  creating space to talk about inequities  in health care is a step forward I know  what it's like to have your voice  silenced and pushed out and I think  because of that I really have a strong  drive to make sure that people don't  experience those same feelings  because I know how painful that can be  and I I really believe 